      "title": "Business Council of Australia more aligned with fossil fuel industry than its members’ values, analysis finds",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/19/bca-policy-energy-companies-members-climate-concerns",
      "description": "<p>Research by InfluenceMap raises questions about association’s policies and representation, particularly around advocacy for new gas supplies</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>The Business Council of Australia is lobbying for gas expansion policies that clash with the climate concerns of the majority of its own members, according to new research that raises questions about the sway of a handful of major companies.</p><p>The business association also does not publicly disclose who is on its energy and climate committee, prompting transparency concerns among researchers over how its policies are shaped and whose interests they represent.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/19/bca-policy-energy-companies-members-climate-concerns\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-18T14:00:02"
    },
    {
      "title": "‘Phantom forests’ in Australia’s arid outback used to create carbon credits meant to offset emissions, report claims",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/18/phantom-forests-in-australian-arid-outback-used-to-create-carbon-credits-meant-to-offset-emissions-report-claims",
      "description": "<p>Carbon credit companies reject research that found only small increase in forest and canopy cover in regeneration areas despite wet conditions</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>A fight over the legitimacy of Australia’s climate change policy has reignited after researchers claimed “phantom forests” in the semi-arid outback are being used to create offsets that allow fossil fuel companies to keep pumping out pollution.</p><p>A group of academics, including professors from the Australian National University and the University of New South Wales, said they had examined 50 forest regeneration projects used to create carbon credits.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/18/phantom-forests-in-australian-arid-outback-used-to-create-carbon-credits-meant-to-offset-emissions-report-claims\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-18T09:30:36"
    },
    {
      "title": "‘I didn’t notice his body being purple’: Byron Bay doula left newborn to inflate a jumping castle, court hears",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/18/byron-bay-doula-left-newborn-to-inflate-jumping-castle-freebirth-inquest-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Inquest into deaths of four babies born in free births or at home in northern NSW between 2022 and 2024 hears ‘something was seriously wrong’</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Baby DH was born in an inflatable pool, in a candle-lit room in his parents’ home near Byron Bay, with no one medically trained present. There are two very different versions of what happened next.</p><p>In the parents’ version, the doula reassured them Baby DH was OK, then left to inflate a jumping castle while their newborn turned purple, became floppy, and struggled to breathe. In the doula’s version, everything seemed OK but she suggested they seek more help if they wanted to.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/18/byron-bay-doula-left-newborn-to-inflate-jumping-castle-freebirth-inquest-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-18T07:58:32"
    },
    {
      "title": "Israel delays building contracts for West Bank settlement after UK sanctions",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/18/israel-construction-contracts-e1-west-bank-settlement-uk-sanctions",
      "description": "<p>Attorney general says E1 settlement decision should not be taken by caretaker government led by Netanyahu</p><p>Israel has postponed issuing contracts for the construction of a large settlement on the West Bank seen as likely to render a two-state solution unviable, days after the UK imposed sanctions against building firms operating in the area.</p><p>Israeli diplomats insisted the move was not in response to <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/17/ed-miliband-israel-west-bank-settlements-sanctions\">the measures from Downing Street</a>. Gali Baharav-Miara, the attorney general, said any decision on settlement expansion should not be taken by the caretaker government led by Benjamin Netanyahu but by whatever new administration was formed.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/18/israel-construction-contracts-e1-west-bank-settlement-uk-sanctions\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-18T14:05:38"
    },
    {
      "title": "‘Godmother’ of illegal Israeli West Bank settler project Daniella Weiss among group sanctioned by Australia",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/18/australia-sanctions-construction-company-west-bank-settlement-expansion-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Foreign affairs minister, Penny Wong, says rapid expansion of settlements and settler violence are ‘extinguishing the possibility of a two-state solution’</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Australia has announced another tranche of sanctions on individuals and organisations involved in “illegal settlement expansion and human rights abuses against Palestinians” in the West Bank.</p><p>In a statement, the foreign affairs minister, Penny Wong, said it was the first time Australian sanctions had targeted a construction company involved in the building of settlements.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/18/australia-sanctions-construction-company-west-bank-settlement-expansion-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
